  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "American Dyeball (Pisolithus arenarius) is a puffball-like fungus belonging to the Pisolithaceae family, characterized by its distinctive structure and historical use as a natural dye source. Despite its common name suggesting dye production, it is primarily known as a mycorrhizal fungus that forms symbiotic relationships with tree roots.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Peridium texture",
      "description": "The outer layer is rough, scaly, and often resembles a bumpy, warty surface that darkens with age.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Interior structure",
      "description": "When cut open, the interior reveals a granular or chambered appearance with distinct pellets or peridioles separated by tissue.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore color",
      "description": "Mature spores appear dark brown to black, and the fruiting body darkens significantly from yellowish-brown to dark brown as it matures.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "American Dyeball thrives in sandy or disturbed soils in New England, often found near pine and oak trees with which it forms mycorrhizal associations.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The common name 'Dyeball' reflects its historical use by textile artisans and dyers who harvested these fungi to create natural dyes, though their practical application was limited compared to other fungal dye sources.",
